The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2254 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2401 (2018) and 2449 (2018), and the statements of its President of 15 February 2013, 2 October 2013, 24 April 2015, 17 August 2015, 18 December 2015, 23 February 2018 and 3 April 2018,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Reaffirming that the Syrian crisis can only be resolved through an inclusive, Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process, facilitated by the United Nations, in accordance with the Geneva Communiqué of 30 June 2012 and resolution 2254 (2015), and stressing the need for the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in all stages of the political process,

Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Syria, in particular in Idlib and northwestern Syria, including the increasing number of civilian casualties, the displacement of civilians, the destruction of civilian infrastructure, and the severe impact on children and other vulnerable persons,

Reaffirming the obligation of all parties to comply with international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precautions, and to respect and protect humanitarian personnel, medical personnel, hospitals, schools and other civilian objects,

Reiterating the need for safe, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all persons in need throughout Syria, including across conflict lines and across borders, and welcoming the efforts of the United Nations and its humanitarian partners in this regard,

Welcoming the efforts of the Russian Federation and Turkey, as guarantors of the Astana process, to reduce violence in the de-escalation zone in Idlib, including the memorandum of 17 September 2018 and the recent joint patrols, and calling for the full implementation of these arrangements,

Reaffirming that terrorism in all its forms and manifestations constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security, and stressing the need to combat by all means, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and international law, threats to international peace and security caused by terrorist acts, including by the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL/Da’esh), Hayat Tahrir al-Sham and other United Nations-listed terrorist groups,

Welcoming the work of the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for Syria and the agreement on the formation of the Constitutional Committee, and looking forward to its early and effective convening in Geneva,

Stressing that there is no military solution to the Syrian conflict, and that the only sustainable solution is a political one, and emphasizing the importance of confidence-building measures, including the release of detainees, the provision of information on missing persons, and the safe and voluntary return of refugees and internally displaced persons,

Recognizing the burden borne by neighbouring States and host communities, and emphasizing the need to ensure the safety and security of United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers,

Bearing in mind the need to prevent further escalation and to create conditions conducive to the full implementation of resolution 2254 (2015),

1. Calls upon all parties to immediately cease hostilities and to exercise maximum restraint throughout Syria, in particular in Idlib and northwestern Syria, and to fully respect and implement the ceasefire arrangements;

2. Reaffirms that the fight against terrorism shall continue, and that the cessation of hostilities referred to in paragraph 1 shall not apply to operations against United Nations-listed terrorist groups, while stressing that all such operations must be conducted in full compliance with international humanitarian law and human rights law;

3. Calls upon all parties to ensure safe, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all persons in need throughout Syria, including in Idlib, Rukban and other hard-to-reach areas, and to facilitate the delivery of humanitarian assistance by the United Nations and its humanitarian partners;

4. Calls upon all parties to protect civilians and civilian infrastructure, including hospitals, schools, water and sanitation facilities, and to respect the principles of medical neutrality and international humanitarian law;

5. Encourages the full and timely implementation of the agreements reached by the guarantors of the Astana process, including the memorandum of 17 September 2018 on the stabilization of the de-escalation zone in Idlib, and welcomes their continued efforts to reduce violence and ensure humanitarian access;

6. Calls upon the parties to take immediate steps to facilitate confidence-building measures, including the release of all persons arbitrarily detained, the provision of information on the fate and whereabouts of missing persons, and the safe, voluntary and dignified return of refugees and internally displaced persons, in accordance with international law;

7. Reaffirms its support for the United Nations-facilitated political process and calls for the early convening of the Constitutional Committee in Geneva, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015), and encourages the Special Envoy to continue his efforts to facilitate a Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Council on the situation in Syria, including the implementation of this resolution, humanitarian access and the political process, within 30 days;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

