Bearing in mind the need to prevent further escalation and to create conditions conducive to the full implementation of resolution 2254 (2015),

1. Demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities and exercise maximum restraint throughout Syria, in particular in Idlib and northwestern Syria, and demands that the current offensive and all military operations and airstrikes, in particular airstrikes against civilians, hospitals, schools, water and sanitation facilities and other civilian infrastructure, immediately end, and that all parties fully respect and implement the ceasefire arrangements;

2. Reaffirms that the fight against terrorism shall continue, and that the cessation of hostilities referred to in paragraph 1 shall not apply to operations against United Nations-listed terrorist groups, provided that such operations are conducted in full compliance with international humanitarian law and human rights law, are targeted and proportionate, and stresses that such operations must not be used as a pretext for targeting civilians or civilian infrastructure or for violating the sovereignty, territorial integrity and unity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and do not relieve any party of its obligations under international humanitarian law;

3. Calls upon all parties to ensure safe, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all persons in need throughout Syria, including in Idlib, Rukban and other hard-to-reach areas, including across conflict lines and through all authorized cross-border crossing points, without arbitrary impediments or undue restrictions by any party, in full respect of the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and in accordance with international law and relevant Security Council resolutions, and to facilitate the delivery of humanitarian assistance by the United Nations and its humanitarian partners in accordance with the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence, and to remove bureaucratic impediments and expedite approvals for humanitarian convoys;

4. Condemns all attacks against civilians and civilian infrastructure, including hospitals, schools, water and sanitation facilities, by all parties, and calls upon all parties to protect civilians and civilian infrastructure and to respect the principles of medical neutrality and international humanitarian law; emphasizes the need to ensure accountability for violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, and calls upon all parties to cooperate with relevant international mechanisms;

5. Condemns any use of chemical weapons in Syria, calls upon all parties to comply fully with the Chemical Weapons Convention and to cooperate fully with the OPCW, and stresses that those responsible for chemical weapons use must be held accountable;

6. Takes note of the efforts of the Russian Federation and Turkey as guarantors of the Astana process and encourages the full and timely implementation of their agreements, including the memorandum of 17 September 2018 on the stabilization of the de-escalation zone in Idlib, in support of and consistent with resolution 2254 (2015) and the United Nations-facilitated Geneva process, while reaffirming that the Astana process should complement and support, and not substitute for, the United Nations-facilitated Geneva process;

7. Calls upon the parties to take immediate steps to facilitate confidence-building measures, including the release of all persons arbitrarily detained, the provision of information on the fate and whereabouts of missing persons, and the safe, voluntary and dignified return of refugees and internally displaced persons, in accordance with international law and international refugee law, including the principle of non-refoulement, and stresses that any return must be voluntary, safe and dignified, that conditions for return must be conducive, and that no refugee or internally displaced person should be forced to return or coerced;

